K PARAGRAPH (1224 chars, 226 words):
Eight kinds of defect to look for in your own review of the plan. Each one says what to look for. A plan may have none of them, one of them or several. (1) A criterion asks for a complete or exhaustive result over a set with no bounded method or stopping rule, or the criterion's scope differs from the scope of the instruction it checks. (2) A rule stated in the driving document is not carried into the plan's task text or criterion. (3) A value, field or state is read, mapped or required by one component and no row, task or step writes it for that case. (4) A criterion embeds a command, pattern or test selector whose literal form cannot match or cannot pass against the text or state the same plan orders. (5) After a change, a count, claim or copy in a second location is left stale because no task enumerates that location. (6) A sentence says "all", "only", "never" or "outside X nothing", and an inventory, the plan's other tasks or the scope of the enforcing test contradict it. (7) Verbatim text the plan dictates omits a clause of a constraint stated elsewhere, or contradicts the plan's own criterion. (8) A plan edits files or behavior that a test elsewhere pins, with no task that updates or runs that test.

